# Build Your First Flow

This tutorial connects Actions into a Flow that prices an invoice and decides how to route it. A Flow is a named graph of Action calls, and Jido derives its run order from the data each component reads.

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, github: "agentjido/jido", branch: "release/v3"}
])
```

## Write the Actions

Each Action does one job. These two come from the same invoice domain as [Build Your First Action](first-action.livemd).

```elixir
defmodule FirstFlow.SumLines do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "first_flow_sum_lines",
    schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{lines: Jido.Joi.list(Jido.Joi.integer())})

  @impl true
  def run(%{lines: lines}, _context), do: {:ok, %{subtotal_cents: Enum.sum(lines)}}
end

defmodule FirstFlow.AddTax do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "first_flow_add_tax",
    schema:
      Jido.Joi.object(%{
        subtotal_cents: Jido.Joi.integer(),
        tax_percent: Jido.Joi.integer()
      })

  @impl true
  def run(%{subtotal_cents: subtotal, tax_percent: tax_percent}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{total_cents: subtotal + div(subtotal * tax_percent, 100)}}
  end
end
```

## Connect them with a Flow

`input(:lines)` reads from the Flow's input. `result("sum", :subtotal_cents)` reads from another component, and that reference makes `"tax"` wait for `"sum"`. `output` is required, and it must come last.

```elixir
defmodule FirstFlow.PriceInvoice do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "first_flow_price_invoice",
    schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{lines: Jido.Joi.list(Jido.Joi.integer())})

  flow do
    step "tax",
      action: FirstFlow.AddTax,
      params: %{subtotal_cents: result("sum", :subtotal_cents), tax_percent: context(:tax_percent)}

    step "sum",
      action: FirstFlow.SumLines,
      params: %{lines: input(:lines)}

    output result("tax")
  end
end

Jido.Exec.run(FirstFlow.PriceInvoice, %{lines: [1_200, 300]}, %{tax_percent: 10})
```

The cell returns `{:ok, %{total_cents: 1650}}`. `"tax"` appears first in the source and still ran second, because source order creates no dependency.

## Read the Flow as data

The module compiles to a `Jido.Flow` value. That value holds each component's target and parameters, and no executable code of its own.

```elixir
%Jido.Flow{name: name, components: components} = FirstFlow.PriceInvoice.flow()

{name, components |> Map.keys() |> Enum.sort()}
```

The cell returns `{"first_flow_price_invoice", ["sum", "tax"]}`.

## Add an inline step and a choice

An inline step holds a small body in the Flow itself. A `choice` runs the Action of the first option whose condition is true, and it needs a fallback. This version sends large invoices to a review queue.

```elixir
defmodule FirstFlow.AssignQueue do
  use Jido.Action, name: "first_flow_assign_queue"

  @impl true
  def run(%{queue: queue, total_cents: total}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{queue: queue, total_cents: total}}
  end
end

defmodule FirstFlow.RouteInvoice do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "first_flow_route_invoice",
    schema:
      Jido.Joi.object(%{
        invoice_id: Jido.Joi.string(),
        lines: Jido.Joi.list(Jido.Joi.integer())
      })

  flow do
    step "sum", action: FirstFlow.SumLines, params: %{lines: input(:lines)}

    step "tax",
      action: FirstFlow.AddTax,
      params: %{subtotal_cents: result("sum", :subtotal_cents), tax_percent: context(:tax_percent)}

    choice "route" do
      option "review",
        condition: result("tax", :total_cents) > 100_000,
        action: FirstFlow.AssignQueue,
        params: %{queue: "review", total_cents: result("tax", :total_cents)}

      otherwise action: FirstFlow.AssignQueue,
                params: %{queue: "auto_approve", total_cents: result("tax", :total_cents)}
    end

    step "summary", invoice_id <- input(:invoice_id) do
      {:ok, %{label: "Invoice " <> invoice_id}}
    end

    output %{
      label: result("summary", :label),
      queue: result("route", :queue),
      total_cents: result("route", :total_cents)
    }
  end
end

context = %{tax_percent: 10}

small = %{invoice_id: "inv-1001", lines: [1_200, 300]}
large = %{invoice_id: "inv-1002", lines: [200_000]}

{:ok, routed_small} = Jido.Exec.run(FirstFlow.RouteInvoice, small, context)
{:ok, routed_large} = Jido.Exec.run(FirstFlow.RouteInvoice, large, context)

{routed_small.queue, routed_large.queue}
```

The cell returns `{"auto_approve", "review"}`. `"summary"` reads only the input, so it does not wait for the other components.

## Handle a failure

Bad input stops the run before any Action starts.

```elixir
{:error, %Jido.Error.ValidationError{details: invalid}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FirstFlow.RouteInvoice, %{invoice_id: 1001, lines: []}, context)

{invalid.phase, Enum.map(invalid.errors, & &1.path)}
```

The cell returns `{:flow_input, [[:invoice_id]]}`.

A missing context value fails when a component needs it. The error names that component.

```elixir
{:error, %Jido.Error.ExecutionError{details: details}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FirstFlow.RouteInvoice, small, %{})

details.node
```

The cell returns `"tax"`.

## The compiler checks the graph

A reference to a component that does not exist fails at compile time. So does a cycle, a duplicate name, or a missing `output`.

```elixir
broken = """
defmodule FirstFlow.Broken do
  use Jido.Flow, name: "first_flow_broken"

  flow do
    step "tax", action: FirstFlow.AddTax, params: %{subtotal_cents: result("missing", :cents)}
    output result("tax")
  end
end
"""

try do
  Code.compile_string(broken)
rescue
  error in CompileError -> Exception.message(error)
end
```

The cell returns a message that ends with `Flow reference points to an unknown component`.

